    "final_answer": "I have found the latest paper in the ArXiv 'Nonlinear Sciences - Chaotic Dynamics' category. Here is the complete information:\n\n## Latest Paper in nlin.CD (Chaotic Dynamics) Category\n\n**Title:** Attractors and basins generated by repeated sums of prime factors of natural numbers\n\n**Authors:** Snehal Shekatkar, Ivano Lodato\n\n**arXiv ID:** arXiv:2609.29232 [nlin.CD]\n\n**Submission Date:** Thursday, 24 September 2026, 08:38:47 UTC\n\n**Subjects:** Chaotic Dynamics (nlin.CD)\n\n**Comments:** 7 pages, 10 figures\n\n### Abstract Summary:\nThe paper investigates the dynamics of the **shifted Alladi-Erdős map**, a one-parameter family of integer maps defined on the natural numbers. In this map, each composite number is mapped to the sum of its prime factors, while each prime is mapped to n + A, where A ∈ ℕ is a fixed shift parameter.\n\n**Key findings:**\n- By systematically exploring the parameter space for 2 ≤ A ≤ 10^5, the authors uncovered a **rich bifurcation structure** characterized by the emergence, disappearance, and reorganization of attractor cycles as the shift parameter varies.\n- Although several attractors may coexist for a given value of A, **almost all natural numbers belong to the basins of only two dominant attractors** for most values of A.\n- The researchers explain the observed bifurcation diagram using **elementary number-theoretic arguments**.\n- They characterize the attractor cycles and quantify the distribution of their basin sizes across the parameter space.\n\n**Conclusion:** The work reveals an unexpectedly rich landscape of arithmetic dynamics arising from a remarkably simple integer map.\n\nThis paper appeared in the ArXiv new submissions listing for Friday, 25 September 2026 (ArXiv lists papers under the date they are announced, which is typically the day after submission).",
